  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warm weather will continue through the week, with \nthe warmest days expected on Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nThe pattern becomes more amplified across the CONUS with a deep \ntrough across the Plains and a building ridge across the east \nthrough midweek. Surface high pressure will persist across the \neastern seaboard and maintain mostly dry weather through Wednesday. \nDuring this time, warm air advection will strengthen across the \nregion. Weak isentropic lift and increasing moisture will support \nwidely scattered showers across western NY Tuesday morning, and \nacross north-central NY late Tuesday morning through Tuesday \nafternoon. Dry air will move back into the region from west to east \nTuesday afternoon through Tuesday night.  \n\nWhile clouds and a few showers impact the region Tuesday, 850mb\ntemperatures are expected to reach +11C. Max temperatures are \nexpected to reach the upper 60s to low 70s. The upper level \nridge is expected to build further north on Wednesday. Another \nvery warm day for early November is expected with maximum \ntemperatures reaching the low to mid 70s. Forecast southerly \nwinds will likely limit lake breeze influences across the \nregion. This setup translates to a very unusual event for the \nregion for early November according to the ECMWF EFI. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather returns to the region late in the \nweek. \n\nA deep trough and closed low will move from the Mid-West to eastern \nCanada late in the work week. A deep southerly flow orginating from \nthe Gulf and the southern Atlantic Ocean will move into the eastern \nGreat Lakes region Wednesday night through Thursday. Forcing from \nthe surface to 500mb and deep moisture will support medium to high \nconfidence for showers across the region. This will provide some \nrelief to the recent dry spell, however confidence in rainfall \namounts remains low. Cooler air will build into the region from west \nto east Thursday, however it will remain warm especially from the \nFinger Lakes region into north-central NY. Cooler weather, although \nstill above normal is expected Friday into the weekend.",
